  {
    "query": "select * from corpus_segments where wylie like ? order by course,seq",
    "parameters": [
      "%'dzin stangs dngos 'gal%"
    ],
    "count": 11,
    "rows": [
      {
        "id": 335,
        "course": "C01",
        "seq": 335,
        "acip": "CES GSUNGS PA LTAR THAR PA THOB PA LA BDAG TU 'DZIN PA RTZA BA NAS SPANG DGOS, DE SPONG BA LA BDAG 'DZIN DANG 'DZIN STANGS DNGOS 'GAL DU GYUR PA'I LAM BDAG MED BSGOM DGOS, BDAG MED BSGOM PA 'DI MED NA SBYIN PA GTONG BA DANG TSUL KHRIMS BSRUNG BA SOGS JI 'DRA ZHIG LA 'BAD KYANG THAR PA 'THOB PAR MI NUS TE, BZHI BRGYA PA LAS, ZHI SGO GNYIS PA YOD MA YIN, , ZHES GSUNGS, DES NA 'KHOR BA LAS THAR BA LA BDAG MED RTOGS PA'I SHES RAB 'DI MED THABS MED PA YIN,",
        "wylie": "ces gsungs pa ltar thar pa thob pa la bdag tu 'dzin pa rtsa ba nas spang dgos, de spong ba la bdag 'dzin dang 'dzin stangs dngos 'gal du gyur pa'i lam bdag med bsgom dgos, bdag med bsgom pa 'di med na sbyin pa gtong ba dang tshul khrims bsrung ba sogs ji 'dra zhig la 'bad kyang thar pa 'thob par mi nus te, bzhi brgya pa las, zhi sgo gnyis pa yod ma yin, , zhes gsungs, des na 'khor ba las thar ba la bdag med rtogs pa'i shes rab 'di med thabs med pa yin",
        "english": "To win freedom you must eliminate, from its root, this grasping to a \"self.\" To eliminate it, you must meditate on how nothing has a self: you must find a path or mental viewpoint which holds things in a way that is completely incompatible with the way you now grasp for a \"self.\" You can make any great efforts you want in practices like charity and morality, but without this meditation on no-self you will never be able to attain freedom. As the 400 Verses says, \"There is no second door to peace.\"146 This wisdom which perceives that no self exists is then a \"without which nothing\" for freeing yourself from the circle of life.",
        "raw": "{\"acip\": \"CES GSUNGS PA LTAR THAR PA THOB PA LA BDAG TU 'DZIN PA RTZA BA NAS SPANG DGOS, DE SPONG BA LA BDAG 'DZIN DANG 'DZIN STANGS DNGOS 'GAL DU GYUR PA'I LAM BDAG MED BSGOM DGOS, BDAG MED BSGOM PA 'DI MED NA SBYIN PA GTONG BA DANG TSUL KHRIMS BSRUNG BA SOGS JI 'DRA ZHIG LA 'BAD KYANG THAR PA 'THOB PAR MI NUS TE, BZHI BRGYA PA LAS, ZHI SGO GNYIS PA YOD MA YIN, , ZHES GSUNGS, DES NA 'KHOR BA LAS THAR BA LA BDAG MED RTOGS PA'I SHES RAB 'DI MED THABS MED PA YIN,\", \"wylie\": \"ces gsungs pa ltar thar pa thob pa la bdag tu 'dzin pa rtsa ba nas spang dgos, de spong ba la bdag 'dzin dang 'dzin stangs dngos 'gal du gyur pa'i lam bdag med bsgom dgos, bdag med bsgom pa 'di med na sbyin pa gtong ba dang tshul khrims bsrung ba sogs ji 'dra zhig la 'bad kyang thar pa 'thob par mi nus te, bzhi brgya pa las, zhi sgo gnyis pa yod ma yin, , zhes gsungs, des na 'khor ba las thar ba la bdag med rtogs pa'i shes rab 'di med thabs med pa yin\", \"english\": \"To win freedom you must eliminate, from its root, this grasping to a \\\"self.\\\" To eliminate it, you must meditate on how nothing has a self: you must find a path or mental viewpoint which holds things in a way that is completely incompatible with the way you now grasp for a \\\"self.\\\" You can make any great efforts you want in practices like charity and morality, but without this meditation on no-self you will never be able to attain freedom. As the 400 Verses says, \\\"There is no second door to peace.\\\"146 This wisdom which perceives that no self exists is then a \\\"without which nothing\\\" for freeing yourself from the circle of life.\", \"course\": \"C01\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 1625,
        "course": "C05",
        "seq": 243,
        "acip": "NYAN RANG DGRA BCOM PA CHOS CAN, PHYI ROL PA BZHIN DU KHAMS GSUM NA SPYOD PA'I PHRA RGYAS THAMS CAD MA SPANGS PAR THAL, DE THAMS CAD KYI RTZA BAR GYUR PA'I DNGOS PO RANG BZHIN GYIS GRUB PAR 'DZIN PA'I RTOG PA DANG 'DZIN STANGS DNGOS 'GAL DU ZHUGS PA'I LAM MA RNYED PA'I PHYIR, DER THAL, DNGOS PO RANG BZHIN MED PAR YONGS SU SHES PA DANG BRAL BA'I PHYIR,",
        "wylie": "nyan rang dgra bcom pa chos can, phyi rol pa bzhin du khams gsum na spyod pa'i phra rgyas thams cad ma spangs par thal, de thams cad kyi rtsa bar gyur pa'i dngos po rang bzhin gyis grub par 'dzin pa'i rtog pa dang 'dzin stangs dngos 'gal du zhugs pa'i lam ma rnyed pa'i phyir, der thal, dngos po rang bzhin med par yongs su shes pa dang bral ba'i phyir",
        "english": "Let us first consider enemy destroyers of the Listener or Self-Made Buddha type. Aren't you saying then that, like the non-Buddhists, they fail to eliminate all the widespread mental afflictions that operate in all three realms? Because isn't it true that they have failed to achieve the path which is directly incompatible, in the way it holds its object, with the root of all these afflictions; that is, the tendency to conceive of things as existing through some nature of their own? And this is true, for they lack that comprehensive knowledge where they realize that things have no nature of their own.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"NYAN RANG DGRA BCOM PA CHOS CAN, PHYI ROL PA BZHIN DU KHAMS GSUM NA SPYOD PA'I PHRA RGYAS THAMS CAD MA SPANGS PAR THAL, DE THAMS CAD KYI RTZA BAR GYUR PA'I DNGOS PO RANG BZHIN GYIS GRUB PAR 'DZIN PA'I RTOG PA DANG 'DZIN STANGS DNGOS 'GAL DU ZHUGS PA'I LAM MA RNYED PA'I PHYIR, DER THAL, DNGOS PO RANG BZHIN MED PAR YONGS SU SHES PA DANG BRAL BA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"nyan rang dgra bcom pa chos can, phyi rol pa bzhin du khams gsum na spyod pa'i phra rgyas thams cad ma spangs par thal, de thams cad kyi rtsa bar gyur pa'i dngos po rang bzhin gyis grub par 'dzin pa'i rtog pa dang 'dzin stangs dngos 'gal du zhugs pa'i lam ma rnyed pa'i phyir, der thal, dngos po rang bzhin med par yongs su shes pa dang bral ba'i phyir\", \"english\": \"Let us first consider enemy destroyers of the Listener or Self-Made Buddha type. Aren't you saying then that, like the non-Buddhists, they fail to eliminate all the widespread mental afflictions that operate in all three realms? Because isn't it true that they have failed to achieve the path which is directly incompatible, in the way it holds its object, with the root of all these afflictions; that is, the tendency to conceive of things as existing through some nature of their own? And this is true, for they lack that comprehensive knowledge where they realize that things have no nature of their own.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 6837,
        "course": "C16",
        "seq": 819,
        "acip": "NYAN RANG DGRA BCOM PA CHOS CAN, PHYI ROL PA BZHIN DU KHAMS GSUM NA SPYOD PA'I PHRA RGYAS THAMS CAD MA SPANGS PAR THAL, DE THAMS CAD KYI RTZA BAR GYUR PA'I DNGOS PO RANG BZHIN GYIS GRUB PAR 'DZIN PA'I RTOG PA DANG 'DZIN STANGS DNGOS 'GAL DU ZHUGS PA'I LAM MA RNYED PA'I PHYIR, DER THAL, DNGOS PO RANG BZHIN MED PAR YONGS SU SHES PA DANG BRAL BA'I PHYIR,",
        "wylie": "nyan rang dgra bcom pa chos can, phyi rol pa bzhin du khams gsum na spyod pa'i phra rgyas thams cad ma spangs par thal, de thams cad kyi rtsa bar gyur pa'i dngos po rang bzhin gyis grub par 'dzin pa'i rtog pa dang 'dzin stangs dngos 'gal du zhugs pa'i lam ma rnyed pa'i phyir, der thal, dngos po rang bzhin med par yongs su shes pa dang bral ba'i phyir",
        "english": "Let us first consider enemy destroyers of the Listener or Self-Made Buddha type. Aren't you saying then that, like the non-Buddhists, they fail to eliminate all the widespread mental afflictions that operate in all three realms? Because isn't it true that they have failed to achieve the path which is directly incompatible, in the way it holds its object, with the root of all these afflictions; that is, the tendency to conceive of things as existing through some nature of their own? And this is true, for they lack that comprehensive knowledge where they realize that things have no nature of their own.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C16\", \"acip\": \"NYAN RANG DGRA BCOM PA CHOS CAN, PHYI ROL PA BZHIN DU KHAMS GSUM NA SPYOD PA'I PHRA RGYAS THAMS CAD MA SPANGS PAR THAL, DE THAMS CAD KYI RTZA BAR GYUR PA'I DNGOS PO RANG BZHIN GYIS GRUB PAR 'DZIN PA'I RTOG PA DANG 'DZIN STANGS DNGOS 'GAL DU ZHUGS PA'I LAM MA RNYED PA'I PHYIR, DER THAL, DNGOS PO RANG BZHIN MED PAR YONGS SU SHES PA DANG BRAL BA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"nyan rang dgra bcom pa chos can, phyi rol pa bzhin du khams gsum na spyod pa'i phra rgyas thams cad ma spangs par thal, de thams cad kyi rtsa bar gyur pa'i dngos po rang bzhin gyis grub par 'dzin pa'i rtog pa dang 'dzin stangs dngos 'gal du zhugs pa'i lam ma rnyed pa'i phyir, der thal, dngos po rang bzhin med par yongs su shes pa dang bral ba'i phyir\", \"english\": \"Let us first consider enemy destroyers of the Listener or Self-Made Buddha type. Aren't you saying then that, like the non-Buddhists, they fail to eliminate all the widespread mental afflictions that operate in all three realms? Because isn't it true that they have failed to achieve the path which is directly incompatible, in the way it holds its object, with the root of all these afflictions; that is, the tendency to conceive of things as existing through some nature of their own? And this is true, for they lack that comprehensive knowledge where they realize that things have no nature of their own.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 35114,
        "course": "DDA",
        "seq": 31,
        "acip": ",BLTOS DANG MI BLTOS DNGOS 'GAL DANG, ,KHYOD KYI BLO DANG NGA YI BLO, ,'DZIN STANGS DNGOS 'GAL ZHUGS PA YIN, ,TSA GRANG LTA BUR 'GAL BA YIN,",
        "wylie": "bltos dang mi bltos dngos 'gal dang, ,khyod kyi blo dang nga yi blo, ,'dzin stangs dngos 'gal zhugs pa yin, ,tsha grang lta bur 'gal ba yin",
        "english": "It's impossible for a single thing To depend on other things and also not Depend on them, at the very same time. The way you think of things, And the way I think of things, Are two incompatible things, Like a single thing hot and cold. In short, it's impossible for the pen to come from me, and not come from me, both at the same time.  The way the Devil sees things and the way the Angel sees things will forever be mutually incompatible.  Again, one of us must leave the mind, forever.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"DDA\", \"acip\": \",BLTOS DANG MI BLTOS DNGOS 'GAL DANG, ,KHYOD KYI BLO DANG NGA YI BLO, ,'DZIN STANGS DNGOS 'GAL ZHUGS PA YIN, ,TSA GRANG LTA BUR 'GAL BA YIN,\", \"wylie\": \"bltos dang mi bltos dngos 'gal dang, ,khyod kyi blo dang nga yi blo, ,'dzin stangs dngos 'gal zhugs pa yin, ,tsha grang lta bur 'gal ba yin\", \"english\": \"It's impossible for a single thing To depend on other things and also not Depend on them, at the very same time. The way you think of things, And the way I think of things, Are two incompatible things, Like a single thing hot and cold. In short, it's impossible for the pen to come from me, and not come from me, both at the same time.  The way the Devil sees things and the way the Angel sees things will forever be mutually incompatible.  Again, one of us must leave the mind, forever.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 35161,
        "course": "DDA",
        "seq": 78,
        "acip": ",DES NA KHYOD DANG NGA GNYIS NI, ,'DZIN STANGS DNGOS 'GAL ZHUGS PA 'DIS, ,LHAN CIG SDOD PA'I GNAS MA MCHIS, ,KHYOD RANG 'GRO BA'I GNAS LA RGYUG",
        "wylie": "des na khyod dang nga gnyis ni, ,'dzin stangs dngos 'gal zhugs pa 'dis, ,lhan cig sdod pa'i gnas ma mchis, ,khyod rang 'gro ba'i gnas la rgyug",
        "english": "Thus the way that you and I See things is completely incompatible; There is no one place in the universe Where both of us could reside. And so it is you who must leave, And go wherever you might. The teachings on incompatibility in Buddhism outline two types.  One is a case where one thing cannot be another, since their very identities are mutually contradictory.  Something cannot be in constant flux, for example, and at the same time be never-changing. The second kind of incompatibility is where two things cannot be in the same place at the same time; say, hot and cold.  The classic, amusing example here since ancient times is the owl and the crow, since they are mortal enemies who will never alight upon the same branch at the same time. Which of these two contradictions applies to the understanding of how the pen comes from seed-pictures, and the misunderstanding that thinks it has always come from its own side?  And which contradiction applies to a pen that came from pictures, and a pen that did not? Sixth Canto 31 Useless Meditations, Whereby the Devil Teaches Us Real Emptiness Meditation The cousin who came for a visit",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"DDA\", \"acip\": \",DES NA KHYOD DANG NGA GNYIS NI, ,'DZIN STANGS DNGOS 'GAL ZHUGS PA 'DIS, ,LHAN CIG SDOD PA'I GNAS MA MCHIS, ,KHYOD RANG 'GRO BA'I GNAS LA RGYUG\", \"wylie\": \"des na khyod dang nga gnyis ni, ,'dzin stangs dngos 'gal zhugs pa 'dis, ,lhan cig sdod pa'i gnas ma mchis, ,khyod rang 'gro ba'i gnas la rgyug\", \"english\": \"Thus the way that you and I See things is completely incompatible; There is no one place in the universe Where both of us could reside. And so it is you who must leave, And go wherever you might. The teachings on incompatibility in Buddhism outline two types.  One is a case where one thing cannot be another, since their very identities are mutually contradictory.  Something cannot be in constant flux, for example, and at the same time be never-changing. The second kind of incompatibility is where two things cannot be in the same place at the same time; say, hot and cold.  The classic, amusing example here since ancient times is the owl and the crow, since they are mortal enemies who will never alight upon the same branch at the same time. Which of these two contradictions applies to the understanding of how the pen comes from seed-pictures, and the misunderstanding that thinks it has always come from its own side?  And which contradiction applies to a pen that came from pictures, and a pen that did not? Sixth Canto 31 Useless Meditations, Whereby the Devil Teaches Us Real Emptiness Meditation The cousin who came for a visit\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 35191,
        "course": "DDA",
        "seq": 108,
        "acip": ",'DZIN STANGS DNGOS 'GAL MA ZHUGS NA, ,NGA NI 'BUD PAR GA LA NUS, ,NGA LA GNOD PAR MA GYUR NA, ,SHES RAB BSGOM PAR GA LA 'GYUR,",
        "wylie": "'dzin stangs dngos 'gal ma zhugs na, ,nga ni 'bud par ga la nus, ,nga la gnod par ma gyur na, ,shes rab bsgom par ga la 'gyur",
        "english": "How on earth can you imagine That you could expel me from this mind Unless you found a viewpoint Which stood in direct opposition To the way that I see things? How could you ever meditate Upon wisdom so long as you Had yet to inflict any damage on me? The concept of one idea “damaging” another is a common and intriguing concept in the study of emptiness.  It just means that we can't possibly think of any particular object in one way and then also think of it in a way that is incompatible with the first. We can't think of something as all blue and then in the same moment think of it as all red.  The one perception “damages” the other. We can't think of any particular object or person as being a composite of pictures coming out of karmic seeds in our own mind, and at the same time think of them as coming towards us from their own side. Useless Meditation #15 Failing to Turn It on Ourselves",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"DDA\", \"acip\": \",'DZIN STANGS DNGOS 'GAL MA ZHUGS NA, ,NGA NI 'BUD PAR GA LA NUS, ,NGA LA GNOD PAR MA GYUR NA, ,SHES RAB BSGOM PAR GA LA 'GYUR,\", \"wylie\": \"'dzin stangs dngos 'gal ma zhugs na, ,nga ni 'bud par ga la nus, ,nga la gnod par ma gyur na, ,shes rab bsgom par ga la 'gyur\", \"english\": \"How on earth can you imagine That you could expel me from this mind Unless you found a viewpoint Which stood in direct opposition To the way that I see things? How could you ever meditate Upon wisdom so long as you Had yet to inflict any damage on me? The concept of one idea “damaging” another is a common and intriguing concept in the study of emptiness.  It just means that we can't possibly think of any particular object in one way and then also think of it in a way that is incompatible with the first. We can't think of something as all blue and then in the same moment think of it as all red.  The one perception “damages” the other. We can't think of any particular object or person as being a composite of pictures coming out of karmic seeds in our own mind, and at the same time think of them as coming towards us from their own side. Useless Meditation #15 Failing to Turn It on Ourselves\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 35240,
        "course": "DDA",
        "seq": 157,
        "acip": ",NGA DANG SHES RAB GNYIS KYI NI, ,'DZIN STANGS DNGOS 'GAL YIN PAS NA, ,'DZIN STANGS DNGOS 'GAL MA ZHUGS PA'I, ,BSGOM GYIS NGA LA GNOD PA MED, ,MUN PA'I GNYEN POR SNANG BA BZHIN, ,DGOS KYI GZHAN GYIS MA YIN NO,",
        "wylie": "nga dang shes rab gnyis kyi ni, ,'dzin stangs dngos 'gal yin pas na, ,'dzin stangs dngos 'gal ma zhugs pa'i, ,bsgom gyis nga la gnod pa med, ,mun pa'i gnyen por snang ba bzhin, ,dgos kyi gzhan gyis ma yin no",
        "english": "They way in which I look at things, And the way that wisdom views them, Are mutually exclusive; And unless you are in a meditation Where the way you look and mine Are mutually exclusive, You do me no damage at all. What we're looking for is a meditation On a real antidote that acts Like a light turned on in the dark— Nothing else will help. As he often does—it seems, mistakenly—the Devil in boasting about the supremacy of his mistaken meditation practices is showing us the true path: We just need to look at things in a way which is completely incompatible with the feeling that they are coming from their own side. It's intriguing to think of the “first discovery” by a suffering human being of this truth that their own mental seeds are pouring forth all their pain.  If the mind is indeed beginningless, then different people for all time have been making this breakthrough.  If they can do it, so can we. Useless Meditation #28 A Mistaken Meditation on Parts",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"DDA\", \"acip\": \",NGA DANG SHES RAB GNYIS KYI NI, ,'DZIN STANGS DNGOS 'GAL YIN PAS NA, ,'DZIN STANGS DNGOS 'GAL MA ZHUGS PA'I, ,BSGOM GYIS NGA LA GNOD PA MED, ,MUN PA'I GNYEN POR SNANG BA BZHIN, ,DGOS KYI GZHAN GYIS MA YIN NO,\", \"wylie\": \"nga dang shes rab gnyis kyi ni, ,'dzin stangs dngos 'gal yin pas na, ,'dzin stangs dngos 'gal ma zhugs pa'i, ,bsgom gyis nga la gnod pa med, ,mun pa'i gnyen por snang ba bzhin, ,dgos kyi gzhan gyis ma yin no\", \"english\": \"They way in which I look at things, And the way that wisdom views them, Are mutually exclusive; And unless you are in a meditation Where the way you look and mine Are mutually exclusive, You do me no damage at all. What we're looking for is a meditation On a real antidote that acts Like a light turned on in the dark— Nothing else will help. As he often does—it seems, mistakenly—the Devil in boasting about the supremacy of his mistaken meditation practices is showing us the true path: We just need to look at things in a way which is completely incompatible with the feeling that they are coming from their own side. It's intriguing to think of the “first discovery” by a suffering human being of this truth that their own mental seeds are pouring forth all their pain.  If the mind is indeed beginningless, then different people for all time have been making this breakthrough.  If they can do it, so can we. Useless Meditation #28 A Mistaken Meditation on Parts\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 35242,
        "course": "DDA",
        "seq": 159,
        "acip": ",RI DANG RA BA KHANG KHYIM DANG, ,RTZIG PA LA NI THAL BYUNG DANG, ,THOGS THUG MED PAR 'GRO BA DANG, ,RDO LA RKANG LAG RJES 'JOG SOGS, ,YON TAN DPAG MED MNGA' MOD KYANG, ,NGA DANG 'DZIN STANGS DNGOS 'GAL GYI, ,GNYEN PO MED PAS NGAR MI GNOD,",
        "wylie": "ri dang ra ba khang khyim dang, ,rtsig pa la ni thal byung dang, ,thogs thug med par 'gro ba dang, ,rdo la rkang lag rjes 'jog sogs, ,yon tan dpag med mnga' mod kyang, ,nga dang 'dzin stangs dngos 'gal gyi, ,gnyen po med pas ngar mi gnod",
        "english": "With this you can walk directly Through the center of a mountain, Or a fence, or building, or a home; No wall can hold you back, What's solid no longer touches you. And then you can also do things Like leaving your footprints Or handprints in solid rock. Oh there are infinite wonders here; But in all of them there's nothing That acts to counter the way In which I look at things— They do me no harm at all. The Devil is mentioning wonders that can be done by a person with exceptional meditational ability that would have been remarkable to people in ancient days.  We can in modern times do our own wonders within our concept of how things work.  That is, science appears to have given us the airplane, the cellphone, the internet. But even as we sit in a plane, or watch an online movie, our mortality is continuing apace.  Unless we learn genuine meditation—emptiness meditation—there will always be a last plane ride, and a final movie.  And then we die and go...where?  Anything that can happen once, can happen again.  That's just plain math.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"DDA\", \"acip\": \",RI DANG RA BA KHANG KHYIM DANG, ,RTZIG PA LA NI THAL BYUNG DANG, ,THOGS THUG MED PAR 'GRO BA DANG, ,RDO LA RKANG LAG RJES 'JOG SOGS, ,YON TAN DPAG MED MNGA' MOD KYANG, ,NGA DANG 'DZIN STANGS DNGOS 'GAL GYI, ,GNYEN PO MED PAS NGAR MI GNOD,\", \"wylie\": \"ri dang ra ba khang khyim dang, ,rtsig pa la ni thal byung dang, ,thogs thug med par 'gro ba dang, ,rdo la rkang lag rjes 'jog sogs, ,yon tan dpag med mnga' mod kyang, ,nga dang 'dzin stangs dngos 'gal gyi, ,gnyen po med pas ngar mi gnod\", \"english\": \"With this you can walk directly Through the center of a mountain, Or a fence, or building, or a home; No wall can hold you back, What's solid no longer touches you. And then you can also do things Like leaving your footprints Or handprints in solid rock. Oh there are infinite wonders here; But in all of them there's nothing That acts to counter the way In which I look at things— They do me no harm at all. The Devil is mentioning wonders that can be done by a person with exceptional meditational ability that would have been remarkable to people in ancient days.  We can in modern times do our own wonders within our concept of how things work.  That is, science appears to have given us the airplane, the cellphone, the internet. But even as we sit in a plane, or watch an online movie, our mortality is continuing apace.  Unless we learn genuine meditation—emptiness meditation—there will always be a last plane ride, and a final movie.  And then we die and go...where?  Anything that can happen once, can happen again.  That's just plain math.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 26545,
        "course": "DTE",
        "seq": 861,
        "acip": "MA KHYAB NA KHYAB PA YOD PAR THAL, SGRA RTAG PAR 'DZIN PA'I RTOG PA DANG, SGRA MI RTAG PAR RTOGS PA'I RJES DPAG GNYIS DMIGS PA GCIG LA DMIGS NAS 'DZIN STANGS DNGOS 'GAL DU ZHUGS PA'I BLO YIN PA'I PHYIR,",
        "wylie": "ma khyab na khyab pa yod par thal, sgra rtag par 'dzin pa'i rtog pa dang, sgra mi rtag par rtogs pa'i rjes dpag gnyis dmigs pa gcig la dmigs nas 'dzin stangs dngos 'gal du zhugs pa'i blo yin pa'i phyir",
        "english": "Just because something is the object that the deductive realization that sound is a changing thing is focusing upon doesn't mean that it has to be the object that the perception which holds that sound is an unchanging thing is focusing upon. Suppose you do say that one doesn't imply the other. And yet it does, because the perception which holds that sound is an unchanging thing, and the deductive realization that sound is a changing thing, are both states of mind that focus upon the same object, and hold it in manners which are in direct contradiction. 10 If a Perception Holds to an Object, Is That an Object Which It Believes In?",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"DTE\", \"acip\": \"MA KHYAB NA KHYAB PA YOD PAR THAL, SGRA RTAG PAR 'DZIN PA'I RTOG PA DANG, SGRA MI RTAG PAR RTOGS PA'I RJES DPAG GNYIS DMIGS PA GCIG LA DMIGS NAS 'DZIN STANGS DNGOS 'GAL DU ZHUGS PA'I BLO YIN PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"ma khyab na khyab pa yod par thal, sgra rtag par 'dzin pa'i rtog pa dang, sgra mi rtag par rtogs pa'i rjes dpag gnyis dmigs pa gcig la dmigs nas 'dzin stangs dngos 'gal du zhugs pa'i blo yin pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"Just because something is the object that the deductive realization that sound is a changing thing is focusing upon doesn't mean that it has to be the object that the perception which holds that sound is an unchanging thing is focusing upon. Suppose you do say that one doesn't imply the other. And yet it does, because the perception which holds that sound is an unchanging thing, and the deductive realization that sound is a changing thing, are both states of mind that focus upon the same object, and hold it in manners which are in direct contradiction. 10 If a Perception Holds to an Object, Is That an Object Which It Believes In?\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 15058,
        "course": "TCS10",
        "seq": 259,
        "acip": "DE YANG PHAR PHYIN THEG PA'I SKABS SU SBYANG GZHI SPYONG BYED RNAM PA MI MTHUN PAR BDAG 'DZIN DANG 'DZIN STANGS DNGOS 'GAL DU GYUR PA'I LAM BDAG MED RTOGS PA'I SHES RAB KYIS BDEN 'DZIN SBYONG BA LTA BU MA YIN PAR,",
        "wylie": "de yang phar phyin theg pa'i skabs su sbyang gzhi spyong byed rnam pa mi mthun par bdag 'dzin dang 'dzin stangs dngos 'gal du gyur pa'i lam bdag med rtogs pa'i shes rab kyis bden 'dzin sbyong ba lta bu ma yin par",
        "english": "When we are studying the way of the perfections, we learn that the thing we wish to purify ourselves of and the thing we use to purify ourselves of it are opposed to each other.  That is, the path we are using--the wisdom which realizes that nothing has any self nature--perceives objects in a way which is in direct contradiction with the way they are perceived by our normal tendency to grasp to some self nature.  The wisdom then purifies us of this tendency to grasp at things as if they truly existed.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"TCS10\", \"acip\": \"DE YANG PHAR PHYIN THEG PA'I SKABS SU SBYANG GZHI SPYONG BYED RNAM PA MI MTHUN PAR BDAG 'DZIN DANG 'DZIN STANGS DNGOS 'GAL DU GYUR PA'I LAM BDAG MED RTOGS PA'I SHES RAB KYIS BDEN 'DZIN SBYONG BA LTA BU MA YIN PAR,\", \"wylie\": \"de yang phar phyin theg pa'i skabs su sbyang gzhi spyong byed rnam pa mi mthun par bdag 'dzin dang 'dzin stangs dngos 'gal du gyur pa'i lam bdag med rtogs pa'i shes rab kyis bden 'dzin sbyong ba lta bu ma yin par\", \"english\": \"When we are studying the way of the perfections, we learn that the thing we wish to purify ourselves of and the thing we use to purify ourselves of it are opposed to each other.  That is, the path we are using--the wisdom which realizes that nothing has any self nature--perceives objects in a way which is in direct contradiction with the way they are perceived by our normal tendency to grasp to some self nature.  The wisdom then purifies us of this tendency to grasp at things as if they truly existed.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 17388,
        "course": "TCS13",
        "seq": 839,
        "acip": "'ON KYANG 'OD GSAL LHAN CIG SKYES PA DE NYID 'KHOR BA'I RTZA BA BDAG 'DZIN DANG 'DZIN STANGS DNGOS 'GAL DU BZHUGS PA MIN PAS DE TZAM GYIS 'KHOR BA LAS MI GROL TE, MGON PO KLU SGRUB KYIS,",
        "wylie": "'on kyang 'od gsal lhan cig skyes pa de nyid 'khor ba'i rtsa ba bdag 'dzin dang 'dzin stangs dngos 'gal du bzhugs pa min pas de tsam gyis 'khor ba las mi grol te, mgon po klu sgrub kyis",
        "english": "However, this inborn clear light by itself does not stand in direct opposition to the root of the cycle of suffering--our tendency to hold to a self-nature of objects we perceive.  And thus with only this we will not be freed from the cycle of suffering.  As the master Arya Nagarjuna has stated:",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"TCS13\", \"acip\": \"'ON KYANG 'OD GSAL LHAN CIG SKYES PA DE NYID 'KHOR BA'I RTZA BA BDAG 'DZIN DANG 'DZIN STANGS DNGOS 'GAL DU BZHUGS PA MIN PAS DE TZAM GYIS 'KHOR BA LAS MI GROL TE, MGON PO KLU SGRUB KYIS,\", \"wylie\": \"'on kyang 'od gsal lhan cig skyes pa de nyid 'khor ba'i rtsa ba bdag 'dzin dang 'dzin stangs dngos 'gal du bzhugs pa min pas de tsam gyis 'khor ba las mi grol te, mgon po klu sgrub kyis\", \"english\": \"However, this inborn clear light by itself does not stand in direct opposition to the root of the cycle of suffering--our tendency to hold to a self-nature of objects we perceive.  And thus with only this we will not be freed from the cycle of suffering.  As the master Arya Nagarjuna has stated:\"}"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "query": "select * from corpus_segments where english like ? order by course,seq",
